nly works with partners that adhere to them.\nL151: Have an idea for a project that will add value for arXiv's community? cite40†Learn more about arXivLabs†info.arxiv.org .\nL152: \nL153: cite41†Which authors of this paper are endorsers? | Disable MathJax (cite42†What is MathJax?†info.arxiv.org )\nL154: \nL155: We gratefully acknowledge support from our major funders, cite43†member institutions†info.arxiv.org , , and all contributors.\nL156: cite44†About†info.arxiv.org · cite45†Help†info.arxiv.org · cite46†Contact†info.arxiv.org · cite47†Subscribe†info.arxiv.org · cite48†Copyright†info.arxiv.org · cite49†Privacy†info.arxiv.org · cite50†Accessibility†info.arxiv.org · cite51†Operational Status (opens in new tab)†status.arxiv.org L157: \nL158: Major funding support from\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nProcedural Knowledge Improves Agentic LLM Workflows (https://arxiv.org/pdf/2511.07568v1)\nciteturn1996view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 weeks ago; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 43; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2511.07568v1\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 1888\nL0@P0: Procedural Knowledge Improves Agentic LLM Workflows\nL1@P0: Vincent Hsiao\nL2@P0: NRC Postdoctoral Fellow, Naval Research Laboratory\nL3@P0: Washington, DC\nL4@P0: United States\nL5@P0: vincent.hsiao.ctr@us.navy.mil\nL6@P0: Mark Roberts\nL7@P0: Navy Center for Applied Research in AI, Naval Research Laboratory\nL8@P0: Washington, DC\nL9@P0: United States\nL10@P0: mark.c.roberts20.civ@us.navy.mil\nL11@P0: Leslie Smith\nL12@P0: Navy Center for Applied Research in AI, Naval Research Laboratory\nL13@P0: Washington, DC\nL14@P0: United States\nL15@P0: leslie.n.smith20.civ@us.navy.mil\nL16@P0: November 12, 2025\nL17@P0: Abstract\nL18@P0: Large language models (LLMs) often struggle when performing agentic tasks without\nL19@P0: substantial tool support, prom-pt engineering, or fine tuning. Despite research showing\nL20@P0: that domain-dependent, procedural knowledge can dramatically increase planning ef\u0002ficiency, little work evaluates its potential for improving LLM performance on agentic\nL21@P0: tasks that may require implicit planning. We formalize, implement, and evaluate an\nL22@P0: agentic LLM workflow that leverages procedural knowledge in the form of a hierarchical\nL23@P0: task network (HTN). Empirical results of our implementation show that hand-coded\nL24@P0: HTNs can dramatically improve LLM performance on agentic tasks, and using HTNs can\nL25@P0: boost a 20b or 70b parameter LLM to outperform a much larger 120b parameter LLM\nL26@P0: baseline. Furthermore, LLM-created HTNs improve overall performance, though less\nL27@P0: so. The results suggest that leveraging expertise–from humans, documents, or LLMs–to\nL28@P0: curate procedural knowledge will become another important tool for improving LLM\nL29@P0: workflows.\nL30@P0: Keywords Large Language Models, Agentic Systems, Task Networks\nL31@P0: 1 Introduction\nL32@P0: Procedural knowledge generally describes a sequence for solving a problem and has played an important\nL33@P0: role in AI. For example, spanning nearly 50 years of automated planning research, procedural knowledge\nL34@P0: has been effective for areas such as decomposing abstract tasks into concrete actions (27; 2), control\nL35@P0: rules for search (1), learning macro operators (6), plan recognition (7), knowledge-based planning (18),\nL36@P0: and planning and acting (8), to name only a few. Procedural knowledge also plays a central role in\nL37@P0: DISTRIBUTION STATEMENT A. Approved for public release; distribution is unlimited.\nL38@P0-1: arXiv:2511.07568v1 [cs.AI] 10 Nov 2025cognitive systems (17), where a specific module in the system manages and applies such knowledge to\nL39@P1: problem solving. Recently, procedural knowledge is used as a kind of meta-strategy to assist in solving\nL40@P1: complex problems (e.g., (3)).\nL41@P1: While LLMs have shown impressive capability, they often perform poorly on planning problems (13), and,\nL42@P1: as we will show later, LLM performance generally degrades as task complexity increases. In complex\nL43@P1: agentic tasks, successful task completion often hinges on an implicit plan that specifies what actions\nL44@P1: to take and in what sequence to take them, a capability that current LLMs often fail to consistently\nL45@P1: complete correctly. Part of the gap is that LLMs are mostly constructed to predict the next token,\nL46@P1: while planning requires managing details that span different temporal horizons and different levels of\nL47@P1: abstraction. LLMs lack a clearly understood way to track the change that results from taking action in\nL48@P1: the world.\nL49@P1: In contrast, this paper examines whether providing explicit procedural knowledge improves LLM\nL50@P1: performance on agentic tasks. To provide an intuitive example (formalized in §3), consider the task of\nL51@P1: booking a travel itinerary. Experienced travelers follow a sequence such as: book flights, book hotel,\nL52@P1: and reserve attractions or restaurants. The sequence might vary by circumstance, but its presence\nL53@P1: makes the problem much easier to solve. The sequence manages dependencies (e.g., confirm flights\nL54@P1: before booking the hotel) and each step narrows the details considered in subsequent steps. But using\nL55@P1: an abstract sequence requires a process that: 1) tracks progress on the overall procedure, 2) maintains\nL56@P1: state changes across steps, 3) takes actions to complete each step, 4) verifies each step is finished before\nL57@P1: proceeding.\nL58@P1: When solving a travel problem like this, LLMs lack the capability to track these layers all at once, leading\nL59@P1: to inefficient or unsuccessful workflows. For example, an LLM might book a restaurant reservation at\nL60@P1: the destination before verifying flight timing, only to backtrack to address such foreseeable conflicts.\nL61@P1: By treating complex tasks as a series of independent decisions rather than attending to interconnected\nL62@P1: dependencies, LLMs struggle to leverage procedural (i.e., hierarchical, goal-oriented) knowledge.\nL63@P1: In this paper, we address two questions: Can we embed procedural knowledge in an LLM workflow? and\nL64@P1: Will procedural knowledge improve LLM performance? We will show that the answer to both questions\nL65@P1: is Yes! To that end:\nL66@P1: • We formalize the problem as an MDP and integrate procedural knowledge using Hierarchical Task\nL67@P1: Networks (HTNs), where abstract tasks decompose into totally ordered subtasks. An HTN ensures\nL68@P1: logical consistency and resource availability.\nL69@P1: • We describe and implement a hybrid LLM-HTN system, ProcLLM, that uses HTNs in an agentic LLM\nL70@P1: workflow. Importantly, this workflow is compatible with any LLM.\nL71@P1: • We evaluate the runtime, success, and number of cycles of ProcLLM on four benchmark problems,\nL72@P1: two from the literature and two synthetic problems. For the problems and HTNs we studied, HTN\nL73@P1: knowledge always improves LLM performance, often significantly.\nL74@P1: • We demonstrate that smaller LLMs benefit more from this workflow and in some cases exceed the\nL75@P1: performance of much larger LLMs without HTN knowledge.\nL76@P1: • Finally, we show that LLM-created HTNs improve performance, though the results are mixed compared\nL77@P1: to hand-coded HTNs.\nL78@P1: Overall, these findings suggest that LLM workflows can benefit considerably from procedural knowledge,\nL79@P1: not only in overall performance but also in a reduced model size and improved response time for the\nL80@P1: same level of performance. Although our implementation uses an Agentic LLM workflow with HTN\nL81@P1: knowledge, the process is straightforward and results may eventually generalize to other LLM workflows,\nL82@P1: so we close with some possible future directions.\nL83@P1: 2 Related Work\nL84@P1: Agentic LLMs. We define agentic LLM as a large language model framework that can act, use tools,\nL85@P1: and plan to autonomously accomplish tasks. Many problems cannot be solved in a single forward pass of\nL86@P1: an LLM, motivating workflow techniques such as prompt chaining (32), where the output of one prompt\nL87@P1: becomes the input to another. Early community systems like AutoGPT (34) showcased the potential of\nL88@P1-2: 2chaining and memory-augmented reasoning for open-ended tasks, while frameworks such as LangChain\nL89@P2: provided modular infrastructure for building agentic pipelines around LLMs\nL90@P2: One of the first academic approaches in this area was the ReAct framework (35), where agent reasoning\nL91@P2: choices are interleaved with action selection in order to interact with external environments. Another\nL92@P2: notable contribution was ExpeL (38), where an agent that learns from experiences and natural language\nL93@P2: to make informed decisions without requiring parametric updates.\nL94@P2: Although there are many variations of this framework, for the purpose of this paper, we will use a\nL95@P2: framework similar to the one used by Reflexion (Fig. 2 in (23)). The Reflexion agent employs an LLM\nL96@P2: (denoted as the agent) and an environment that processes actions across multiple iterations. At each\nL97@P2: iteration, the agent receives an observation from the environment. This observation is combined with a\nL98@P2: prompt given to the LLM to output some action. The environment processes this action and then returns\nL99@P2: the observation for the next iteration.\nL100@P2: Perhaps the closest to this paper is the concurrent work of Belcak and Molchanov (3). In their universal\nL101@P2: deep research (UDR) framework, a strategy compiler is used to take a research strategy from the user\nL102@P2: and compile it into a list of steps and corresponding outputs that a deep research tool needs to execute\nL103@P2: step by step. The structure is enforced by incorporating code comments and yield directives that specify\nL104@P2: the goal of each step and the intended outputs of the step. Our approach is more general, and we\nL105@P2: can hypothesize that there exists a task network that when applied to our system would replicate the\nL106@P2: behavior of UDR.\nL107@P2: LLMs and Tool-use. To supplement capabilities in domains where LLMs have poor performance, it is\nL108@P2: common to allow LLMs to interface with external tools. These tools enhance the LLMs with capabilities\nL109@P2: that they might otherwise not have (31) and there are numerous papers covering various aspects of this\nL110@P2: field of research (12; 20). Early demonstrations included Toolformer (21), which fine-tuned LLMs to\nL111@P2: invoke APIs from demonstrations, and MRKL (15), which integrated symbolic reasoning with tool-use.\nL112@P2: Unlike past work, our system allows the LLM agent to write arbitrary code to call arbitrary APIs. This is\nL113@P2: different from works where the external tools are presented in a standardized in-context format to an\nL114@P2: LLM for usage (e.g., MCP servers). Allowing the LLM to write arbitrary code also gives more flexibility\nL115@P2: in problem solving (e.g., coding allows the LLM to embed tool calls in algorithms), which standardized\nL116@P2: tool formats do not permit.\nL117@P2: Multi-agent Systems. The extension from single-agent to multi-agent frameworks has become an\nL118@P2: important line of research. In AgentOrchestra (37), an orchestrator agent creates a plan that is then\nL119@P2: executed by specialized sub-agents. Unlike our explicit task network representation, AgentOrchestra\nL120@P2: uses a simple list structure. SagaLLM (4) proposes a transactional system to handle multi-step planning\nL121@P2: in multi-agent LLM systems. Other works, such as CAMEL (19), demonstrated emergent coordination\nL122@P2: between specialized LLM agents by role-playing tasks and negotiating via natural language.\nL123@P2: Hybrid Systems. Various hybrid systems have been developed for application to specific domains.\nL124@P2: For example, (11) provides a survey of LLM-based agentic recommenders that incorporate subsystems\nL125@P2: similar to planning and acting modules. AutoConcierge (36) employs LLMs to convert user requests\nL126@P2: into formalized representations to be used by a reasoner. This is also similar to the approach from (10),\nL127@P2: where user requests and API calls are translated into constraints for an SMT solver. Both approaches\nL128@P2: use LLMs to convert user data into formalized problem descriptions, which are then solved to provide a\nL129@P2: solution. This is different from our approach, which instead uses a formalized representation to assist\nL130@P2: the LLM in the problem-solving process.\nL131@P2: Other than agentic LLM systems, there are many LLM-modulo systems that combine LLMs with external\nL132@P2: verifiers and or solvers (14). ISR-LLM (39) employs an LLM to translate planning problems in natural\nL133@P2: language into PDDL and then iteratively calls the external planner to verify LLM-generated plans.\nL134@P2: Beyond agentic LLM systems, many hybrid approaches combine LLMs with external verifiers or solvers\nL135@P2: (14). ISR-LLM (39), for instance, employs an LLM to translate planning problems in natural language\nL136@P2: into PDDL and then iteratively calls an external planner to verify and refine plans. Similarly, program\nL137@P2: synthesis approaches (5) use LLMs to generate candidate code that is executed and verified externally.\nL138@P2-3: 3These hybrid formulations highlight a broader paradigm: using LLMs as flexible natural-language\nL139@P3: interfaces while delegating correctness and reliability to symbolic or algorithmic components.\nL140@P3: LLMs and Planning. In challenging tasks, an agent may need to generate a plan before completing\nL141@P3: the task. Many researchers have investigated how LLMs can be used effectively for planning (26) (25)\nL142@P3: (14). There have been impressive gains in this area of research; for example, a recent notable example\nL143@P3: is by Verma et al. (30) which claims up to 94% planning accuracy using a fine-tuning approach paired\nL144@P3: with chain-of-thought reasoning. Despite these gains, planning remains a challenging task (24) (29)\nL145@P3: with many results showing that LLMs still have many weaknesses when applied to these problems.\nL146@P3: Furthermore, many approaches still require considerable scaffolding.\nL147@P3: A consistent claim is that the effectiveness of LLM planning is reduced as task complexity increases.\nL148@P3: Kambhampati (13) argues that LLMs engage in universal approximate retrieval, where they rely on\nL149@P3: pattern matching rather than systematic reasoning. This suffices for generating immediate solutions or\nL150@P3: short inference chains, but can run into issues when task complexity increases, which can be seen in\nL151@P3: ACPBench (16) where the task of generating just the next action has a much higher success rate than\nL152@P3: the more complex tasks. This suggests that current LLMs are most effective at solving short tasks that\nL153@P3: do not need multi-step planning. A natural solution would be as follows: When the provided task is too\nL154@P3: complex for an LLM agent, decompose the task into subtasks for which the LLM is effective.\nL155@P3: Unlike other works in this area, our focus is not directly on the ability of an LLM to produce a plan\nL156@P3: (i.e., a sequence of steps solving the planning task). Rather, in many tasks that agentic LLMs are\nL157@P3: applied to solve, a sort of meta-planning is a requisite process for solving complex problem instances.\nL158@P3: By decomposing a task into smaller subtasks, the meta-planning required will be less complex and the\nL159@P3: agentic system will be less impacted by weaknesses of current LLMs when encountering complex tasks.\nL160@P3: 3 Preliminaries\nL161@P3: We represent procedural knowledge using a Hierarchical Task Network (HTN), described next. We then\nL162@P3: formalize the problem as a Markov Decision Process (MDP) where the policy that solves the MDP is an\nL163@P3: LLM. Finally, we analyze why we expect ProcLLM to perform well.\nL164@P3: Hierarchical Task Networks (HTNs). Because our focus is on how procedural knowledge improves\nL165@P3: LLM performance, our description of HTNs sacrifices exact formality for a more notional understanding.\nL166@P3: A full formal treatment can be found in (8). Let K be a set of tasks; a task κ ∈ K is a labeled name\nL167@P3: of something the system needs to do. Figure 1 (top) shows an HTN decomposition tree where K\nL168@P3: relates to the travel problem from §1. HTNs decompose complex tasks (white boxes) into primitive\nL169@P3: tasks (gray boxes) using methods (ovals). Complex tasks describe abstract tasks that need to be\nL170@P3: decomposed and primitive tasks describe concrete executable tasks. Figure 1 shows two methods.\nL171@P3: Method m-process-user-request decomposes κp into ⟨κ1, .., κ4⟩, where the κ1 (dashed box) has passed\nL172@P3: the verify check (described below). Method choose departing flight decomposes κ2 into primitive\nL173@P3: tasks: ⟨understand flight tool, write flight to notes⟩.\nL174@P3: For a set of methods M, a method m ∈ M is a tuple (head, task, pre, subtasks), where the head is the\nL175@P3: name and any paramaters of the method, task matches the taskname the method can decompose, pre\nL176@P3: is a (possibly empty) set of preconditions for applying the method in a state, and subtasks is a totally\nL177@P3: ordered sequence of tasks ⟨κ1, κ2, .., κn⟩. A method that matches a task is relevant, and HTN planners\nL178@P3: often select the first relevant method in M (As described in §4, ProcLLM’s Algorithm 1, Line 24 uses\nL179@P3: FindFirstRelevantMethod to select methods).\nL180@P3: Markov Decision Process (MDP) Model . We formalize agentic tasks as a finite-horizon deterministic\nL181@P3: Markov Decision Process (MDP), defined by the tuple M = (S, A, T , r, H), where S is the set of states\nL182@P3: with a subset of absorbing terminal states, Sterm ⊂ S, A is set of actions, T : S × A → S is a deterministic\nL183@P3: state transition function, where si+1 = T (si, ai), r : S × A → R is a reward function, and H is a finite\nL184@P3: horizon. An episode terminates either by reaching a terminal state or by exceeding the horizon (timeout).\nL185@P3-4: 41 {\"m-travel-process-user-request\": {\nL186@P4: 2 \"task\": \"process user request\",\nL187@P4: 3 \"subtasks\": {\"1\": \"understand user request\",\nL188@P4: 4 \"2\": \"choose a departing flight\",\nL189@P4: 5 \"3\": \"choose a returning flight\",\nL190@P4: 6 \"4\": \"choose an accommodation\" },\nL191@P4: 7 \"verify\": \"answer.txt contains a solution in the correct format that follows the problem specification\",\nL192@P4: 8 \"verify_files\": {\"file1\": \"problem_specification.txt\",\nL193@P4: 9 \"file2\": \"answer.txt\" }},\nL194@P4: 10 \"m-choose-departing-flight\": {\nL195@P4: 11 \"task\": \"choose a departing flight\",\nL196@P4: 12 \"subtasks\": {\"1\": \"understand flights tool\",\nL197@P4: 13 \"2\": \"write flight to notes\" },\nL198@P4: 14 \"verify\": \"notes contains details about a flight number and cost for a flight from origin to destination on departure\nL199@P4: date consistent with request preferences\",\nL200@P4: 15 \"verify_files\": {\"file1\": \"files/notes.txt\",\nL201@P4: 16 \"file2\": \"files/problem_specification.txt\",\nL202@P4: 17 \"file3\": \"files/request.txt\" } },\nL203@P4: 18 \"m-understand-flights-tool\": {\nL204@P4: 19 \"name\": \"understand flights tool\",\nL205@P4: 20 \"effect\": \"notes contains details about flights tool methods inputs and outputs\",\nL206@P4: 21 \"effect_files\": {\"file1\": \"files/notes.txt\",\nL207@P4: 22 \"file2\": \"files/tools_specification.txt\" } } }\nL208@P4: Figure 1: Top: An in-progress HTN decomposition tree for the Travel Planner problem of §1. White boxes\nL209@P4: indicate abstract tasks, round ovals indicate methods that decompose tasks, and gray boxes indicate\nL210@P4: primitive tasks for the LLM to execute. The dashed box for κ1 indicates it has passed verify. Bottom:\nL211@P4: Notional methods for the same problem.\nL212@P4-5: 5For our domains, we define a function verify : S → {0, 1}, that identifies terminal states (i.e.,\nL213@P5: verify(s) = 1 if s ∈ Sterm and 0 otherwise). The reward function is then:\nL214@P5: r(s,a) = (\nL215@P5: rsuccess if verify(T (s,a)) = 1 (a terminal state)\nL216@P5: rstep otherwise\nL217@P5: where rsuccess = 1 is a positive reward for task completion and rstep is a negative reward for non-terminal\nL218@P5: steps (e.g., rstep = −0.1). Primitive tasks from the HTN are linked to Sterm, which can be verified using\nL219@P5: verify.\nL220@P5: An agent’s behavior is described by a stochastic policy π : S×A → [0, 1], where π(s, a) = P(ai = a | si = s)\nL221@P5: is the probability of selecting a in state s. A solution to M is a policy π that maximizes the expected\nL222@P5: reward (in this case a policy that reaches a terminal state in the fewest steps).\nL223@P5: Initial Analysis. In this work, we investigate the use of an agentic LLM system as the policy π that\nL224@P5: operates within this MDP framework. We show that divide-and-conquer mechanisms such as task\nL225@P5: decomposition can be used to constrain π to work on smaller problems, increasing the expected reward\nL226@P5: obtained by our agentic LLM.\nL227@P5: The general MDP model allows us to hypothesize about why we expect this approach to work. We assume\nL228@P5: that there exists a task-aware assembly function A : S × K → C and let Ci be the context produced from\nL229@P5: state si used as input to the LLM at each iteration.\nL230@P5: Task Success Rate. A task succeeds if a terminal state s ∈ Sterm is reached within H steps. Let\nL231@P5: (a0, . . . , aH−1) be a sequence of actions that arrives at a terminal state from an initial state s0. As\u0002suming deterministic transitions, the probability of taking these actions depends solely on the LLM’s\nL232@P5: parameterization θ, which governs action selection:\nL233@P5: Pθ(a0, a1, . . . , aH−1) =\nL234@P5: H\nL235@P5: Y−1\nL236@P5: i=0\nL237@P5: Pθ(ai|Ci)\nL238@P5: Here, Pθ(ai|Ci) represents the LLM’s conditional probability of selecting action ai given context Ci.\nL239@P5: This factorization arises because each action depends only on the current context (Markov property).\nL240@P5: Task Decomposition. A task decomposition splits a problem into a sequence of smaller subtasks,\nL241@P5: (κ0, . . . , κn−1). Let Pθ(κj ) be the probability of successfully completing subtask j. Decomposition\nL242@P5: improves task success if:\nL243@P5: Pθ(full task) <\nL244@P5: nY−1\nL245@P5: j=0\nL246@P5: Pθ(κj )\nL247@P5: We hypothesize that we can improve the right side of this equation through contextual scaffolding. We\nL248@P5: assume that the LLM’s ability to complete a subtask Pθ(κj ) increases by reducing context complexity.\n"
